  "notes": "TRANSITIVITY:\nIntransitive. The fruit ripens by itself: {桃|もも}が{熟|う}れる. To say that someone ripens fruit, use {熟|じゅく}させる or {追熟|ついじゅく}させる.\n\nASPECT:\n{熟|う}れている normally describes the resulting state ('is ripe'), not an action in progress, so it is the form learners meet most often. {熟|う}れる on its own points to the future or to a general truth.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- よく{熟|う}れた: nicely ripened\n- {熟|う}れた{果物|くだもの}: ripe fruit\n- {熟|う}れすぎる: to become overripe\n- まだ{熟|う}れていない: not ripe yet\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {熟|じゅく}す: to ripen — same meaning, more formal, and also used figuratively of a time being ripe ({機|き}が{熟|じゅく}す)\n- {実|みの}る: to bear fruit — focuses on fruit forming, and is common figuratively for efforts paying off\n- {売|う}れる: to sell — an unrelated homophone; the kanji tells them apart",
